{"title":"Gauss-Bodenmiller Theorem","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>Gauss-Bodenmiller Theorem</b> is a theorem in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometry\" title=\"Geometry\">geometry</a> concerning <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complete_Quadrilateral\" title=\"Complete Quadrilateral\">complete quadrilaterals</a> and the circles on its diagonals.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Statement\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Statement</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proof_Sketch\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof Sketch</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Statement\">Statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss-Bodenmiller_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><i>Consider quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/4/d54b87f3859b616e801a8e8996dc0055f3346624.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P=AD \\cap BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"119\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/3/c8328c2ff628cdec51c57d664ef0bd717ba3f9f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q = AB \\cap CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"119\" height=\"18\" />. Then, the circles with diameters <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/12517bbc31cb9026c71299b7a0d232d07e60f284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/7/4373e7b4be983c0315e82e1fda1df66ed34dd7fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BD}$\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deaae00f6af4ac1f5882568e7b6667bb334a2ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{PQ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"29\" height=\"19\" /> are coaxial. Their radical axis is a line passing through each of the four orthocenters of the triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/5/4f550db59d15933de1a0871aaa917acdd99966af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PAB$\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/a/f0a0a4554dcbef4be00a35d49eff85301adc911b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PCD$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/f/80fcfbfb9fa7b927789f75e97669d9806d51b7f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$QAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/2/8b2df9c76dad74e8292f75b59179bf85aca32ebd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$QBC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />.</i>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_Sketch\">Proof Sketch</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss-Bodenmiller_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof Sketch\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The theorem looks complicated, but the proof is quite simple. The idea is to just take every orthocenter and proves that it has the same power with respect to all three circles. Then, the orthocenters lie on the radical axes, which implies the conclusion.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss-Bodenmiller_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/d/05d0bb1172cf128548aec51cabd885258e296e6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> denote the circles with diameters <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deaae00f6af4ac1f5882568e7b6667bb334a2ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{PQ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"29\" height=\"19\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/12517bbc31cb9026c71299b7a0d232d07e60f284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/7/4373e7b4be983c0315e82e1fda1df66ed34dd7fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BD}$\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" />, respectively.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/0/d307bbd1badc9884ef8f01724a6e1cbf9e173a8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> denote the orthocenter of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/1/ac1c6112376b5d18ec9bcc888a024b6837279f51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"18\" />. It is not hard to see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/0/d307bbd1badc9884ef8f01724a6e1cbf9e173a8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> is the radical center of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> and the circle with diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/3/09372d25176f61fe3add21a02fa9fbcf936009c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{QC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"31\" height=\"21\" />: note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, the foot of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" />-altitude, foot of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />-altitude are concyclic with diamter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/c/49c8e8b86b75be6552b89161d494582401bec3fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$QC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> and foot of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" />-altitude lie on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and foot of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />-altitude lie on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/0/d307bbd1badc9884ef8f01724a6e1cbf9e173a8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> lies on the radical axis of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />. Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/0/d307bbd1badc9884ef8f01724a6e1cbf9e173a8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> lies on the radical axes of all three pairs, so it is the radical center.\n</p><p>Similarly, the other three orthocenters also have the same properties, and this is only possible if the radical axes of all three pairs coincide, as desired. Therefore, all four orthocenters are collinear, and they lie on the so called <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Steiner_line\" title=\"Steiner line\">Steiner line</a>.\n</p><p>Note that this also proves the existence of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss_line\" title=\"Gauss line\">Gauss line</a>, which is defined as the line passing through the midpoints of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/12517bbc31cb9026c71299b7a0d232d07e60f284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/7/4373e7b4be983c0315e82e1fda1df66ed34dd7fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BD}$\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deaae00f6af4ac1f5882568e7b6667bb334a2ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{PQ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"29\" height=\"19\" />, which are just the centers of the three circles, which lies perpendicular to the Steiner Line.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss-Bodenmiller_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Miquel%27s_point\" title=\"Miquel&#39;s point\">Miquel's point</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Simson_line\" title=\"Simson line\">Simson line</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Steiner_line\" title=\"Steiner line\">Steiner line</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss_line\" title=\"Gauss line\">Gauss line</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss-Bodenmiller_Theorem&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018085346\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.022 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.037 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 222/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 316/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6931/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.742      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.742      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:33065:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018085346 and revision id 254931. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}